\begin{abstract}
GRPO-trained formalizers converge to high prove rates on FOLIO ($95{\pm}5\%$, 3 seeds) yet produce effectively zero structurally sound outputs (0--8\% genuine across seeds at convergence), a failure invisible to execution-based evaluation.
We propose \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), applying leave-one-out necessity testing to solver inputs.
The resulting metric (ISR) exposes a 99.5\% structural collapse hidden behind rising prove rates; a supervised baseline achieves 92\% non-hacking on identical data, localizing the failure to RL.
Repurposed as a reward signal, ISR provides an empirical instance of Goodhart's Law~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: it significantly amplifies tautological gaming versus a matched random control ($\text{OR}{=}15$, $p{<}10^{-39}$), and the sole apparent benefit vanishes ($1.06{\times}$) after controlling for a premise-count confound.
Gaming dominates across all four dose levels tested; the effect transfers to Prolog (3/3 seeds, 0\% non-hacking) and across model scale (Qwen3-1.7B discovers a qualitatively novel gaming mode absent in 4B).
Across 6 conditions, 2 logic types, 2 model scales, and 3 seeds, we catalog 9 hacking modes and document ISR's fundamental asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, unreliable as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}

tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
GRPO-trained formalizers converge to high prove rates on FOLIO ($95{\pm}5\%$, 5 seeds) yet produce effectively zero structurally sound outputs at convergence, a failure invisible to execution-based evaluation.
We propose \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), applying leave-one-out necessity testing to solver inputs.
The resulting metric (ISR) exposes a 99.5\% structural collapse hidden behind rising prove rates; a supervised baseline achieves 92\% non-hacking on identical data, localizing the failure to RL.
Repurposed as a reward signal, ISR contains an inherent Goodhart vulnerability: tautological proofs receive artificially high ISR (${\geq}0.5$ by construction), creating a positive feedback loop that guarantees eventual collapse at all tested doses ($\beta\in\{0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3\}$).
At moderate doses, collapse is stochastic and delayed, producing a transient window where genuine reasoning coexists with gaming (${\sim}23\%$ genuine at 500 steps, 5 seeds); trajectory analysis confirms this window closes as all seeds converge to gaming ($17\%{\to}2\%$ genuine, 1000 steps).
The effect transfers to Prolog (3/3 seeds, 0\% non-hacking) and across model scale (Qwen3-1.7B discovers qualitatively novel gaming modes absent in 4B).
Across 6 conditions, 2 logic types, 2 model scales, and 5 seeds, we catalog 9 hacking modes and document that $\beta$ controls \emph{when} collapse occurs, not \emph{whether}: ISR is reliable as a diagnostic but unreliable as an optimization target at any dose.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Contributions.}
Across 6 reward conditions, 3 seeds, 2 logic types, and up to 1000 steps, we contribute: (\textbf{C1}) ISR as a diagnostic for GRPO reward hacking, with a 99.5\% collapse trajectory invisible to PR and an SFT control localizing the failure to RL (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); (\textbf{C2}) an empirical instance of Goodhart's Law~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy} where a diagnostic is gamed when optimized: a random control shows ISR significantly amplifies tautological gaming ($\text{OR}{=}15$, $p{<}10^{-38}$; 3-seed matched), a dose-response across four $\beta$ values shows gaming dominates at every level, and premise-count-matched analysis shows the sole apparent gain is an LOO artifact (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}); (\textbf{C3}) cross-logic transfer to Prolog with consistent Goodhart effects across all seeds (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}); (\textbf{C4}) a nine-mode hacking taxonomy including query-as-fact gaming, obfuscated tautology, and a scale-specific \emph{ex falso} mode, motivating functional cross-validation of diagnostics (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).
These are cautionary results for any work treating a structural proxy as a training signal.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Contributions.}
Across 6 reward conditions, 5 seeds, 2 logic types, 2 model scales, and up to 1000 steps, we contribute: (\textbf{C1}) ISR as a diagnostic for GRPO reward hacking, with a 99.5\% collapse trajectory invisible to PR and an SFT control localizing the failure to RL (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); (\textbf{C2}) an empirical instance of inevitable Goodhart collapse~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: ISR's structural bias toward tautology ($\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ for $P{\to}P$) creates a positive feedback loop that causes gaming at all four tested doses; collapse is stochastic and delayed at moderate $\beta$ but trajectory analysis confirms all seeds converge, showing that $\beta$ controls timing, not outcome (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}); (\textbf{C3}) cross-logic transfer to Prolog with consistent Goodhart effects across all seeds (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}); (\textbf{C4}) a nine-mode hacking taxonomy including query-as-fact gaming, obfuscated tautology, and a scale-specific \emph{ex falso} mode, motivating functional cross-validation of diagnostics (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).
These are cautionary results: snapshot evaluation of structural rewards can overestimate their benefit; only trajectory analysis reveals inevitable collapse.

